## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design runtime-extensible logic with proper isolation and performance, combining Strategy, Registry, and caching patterns in a multi-tenant setup.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern with runtime plug-ins
- Registry for dynamic discovery and hot-loading
- Per-tenant isolation in evaluation and caching
- Async evaluation paths for complex rules
- TypeScript type safety and generics for contexts

## Code Example

```typescript
interface EvaluationContext {
  tenantId: string
  userId?: string
  attributes?: Record<string, any>
}

interface DecisionStrategy {
  evaluate(flagKey: string, ctx: EvaluationContext): Promise<boolean>
}

class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, new () => DecisionStrategy>()
  register(name: string, ctor: new () => DecisionStrategy) {
    this.map.set(name, ctor)
  }
  resolve(name: string): DecisionStrategy {
    const Ctor = this.map.get(name)
    if (!Ctor) throw new Error(`Unknown strategy: ${name}`)
    return new Ctor()
  }
}

class Cache {
  // simple TTL cache interface
  get(key: string) { /* ... */ }
  set(key: string, value: boolean, ttl?: number) { /* ... */ }
}

class FlagEngine {
  constructor(private registry: Registry, private cache: Cache) {}
  async isEnabled(flagKey: string, ctx: EvaluationContext): Promise<boolean> {
    const cacheKey = `${ctx.tenantId}:${flagKey}`
    const cached = this.cache.get(cacheKey)
    if (cached !== undefined) return cached
    // strategy name could come from a central flag config; simplified here
    const strategyName = 'percent'
    const strategy = this.registry.resolve(strategyName)
    const result = await strategy.evaluate(flagKey, ctx)
    this.cache.set(cacheKey, result, 300000)
    return result
  }
}
```

