## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a runtime-extensible component using well-established patterns—Strategy and Registry—and to reason about concurrency and distribution boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- **Strategy Pattern**: Enables pluggable eviction and serialization policies
- **Registry Pattern**: Allows dynamic extension by name without rebuilding
- **Thread-Safety**: Concurrent access with safe eviction operations
- **Per-Cache Isolation**: Independent policy and serializer instances per cache
- **Distributed Invalidation**: Cache coherence across nodes

## Code Example
```typescript
// Core interfaces
interface EvictionPolicy<K, V> {
  onAccess(key: K): void;
  selectEvictionCandidate(): K | null;
  onEvict(key: K): void;
}

interface Serializer<V> {
  serialize(value: V): Uint8Array;
  deserialize(data: Uint8Array): V;
}

interface Cache<K, V> {
  get(key: K): V | undefined;
  put(key: K, value: V): void;
  evict(key: K): void;
  clear(): void;
}

// Registry for runtime extension
class PolicyRegistry<K, V> {
  private policies = new Map<string, new () => EvictionPolicy<K, V>>();
  register(name: string, policy: new () => EvictionPolicy<K, V>): void {
    this.policies.set(name, policy);
  }
  resolve(name: string): EvictionPolicy<K, V> {
    const Policy = this.policies.get(name);
    if (!Policy) throw new Error(`Unknown policy: ${name}`);
    return new Policy();
  }
}

class SerializerRegistry<V> {
  private serializers = new Map<string, new () => Serializer<V>>();
  register(name: string, serializer: new () => Serializer<V>): void {
    this.serializers.set(name, serializer);
  }
  resolve(name: string): Serializer<V> {
    const Serializer = this.serializers.get(name);
    if (!Serializer) throw new Error(`Unknown serializer: ${name}`);
    return new Serializer();
  }
}

// Usage
const policyRegistry = new PolicyRegistry<string, User>();
policyRegistry.register('lru', LRUPolicy);
policyRegistry.register('lfu', LFUPolicy);

const serializerRegistry = new SerializerRegistry<User>();
serializerRegistry.register('json', JsonSerializer);
serializerRegistry.register('protobuf', ProtobufSerializer);

const cache = new DefaultCache<string, User>({
  policy: policyRegistry.resolve('lru'),
  serializer: serializerRegistry.resolve('json'),
  maxSize: 1000,
});
```

## Thread-Safety Considerations
- **Concurrent Map**: Use a thread-safe map (e.g., `ConcurrentHashMap` in Java, or mutexes in TypeScript/Node.js)
- **Atomic Operations**: Ensure get/put are atomic to prevent race conditions during eviction
- **Lock-Free Structures**: Consider lock-free data structures for high-throughput scenarios
- **Read/Write Locks**: Separate read and write locks to maximize concurrency

## Per-Cache Isolation
- Each cache instance maintains its own policy and serializer instances
- Policies operate on isolated internal state, preventing cross-cache interference
- Cache namespacing prevents key collisions in shared registries

## Distributed Invalidation
- **Write-Through/Write-Behind**: Propagate updates to a central store (Redis, Cassandra)
- **Pub/Sub Invalidation**: Subscribe to invalidation events on a message broker (Kafka, Redis Pub/Sub)
- **Versioning/Tombstones**: Track cache generations or deleted keys to prevent stale reads
- **Consistent Hashing**: Ensure requests route to nodes holding the cached entry for targeted invalidation

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a runtime-extensible plugin architecture for critical control logic. It tests understanding of extensibility patterns, safety considerations, and observability requirements under high-load production environments similar to those at Netflix, Tesla, or Oracle.

## Key Concepts
- **Strategy Pattern**: Encapsulates each evaluation algorithm (Boolean, Percentage, UserSegment) behind a common interface
- **Registry/Plugin Loader**: Maintains a map of evaluator names to implementations, enabling runtime registration without redeployment
- **Hot-Reloadable Modules**: Allows new evaluators to be added or updated without restarting the service
- **Per-Flag Isolation**: Each flag has dedicated configuration and metrics to prevent cascade failures
- **Observability**: Built-in logging, metrics, and audit trails for every flag evaluation

## Code Example
```typescript
interface FlagEvaluationContext {
  userId?: string;
  attributes?: Record<string, any>;
  [key: string]: any;
}

interface FlagConfig {
  key: string;
  strategy: string;
  parameters?: Record<string, any>;
  enabled?: boolean;
}

interface IFlagEvaluator {
  name: string;
  evaluate(config: FlagConfig, context: FlagEvaluationContext): boolean;
}

class FlagRegistry {
  private evaluators = new Map<string, IFlagEvaluator>();
  private cache = new Map<string, IFlagEvaluator>();

  register(evaluator: IFlagEvaluator): void {
    this.evaluators.set(evaluator.name, evaluator);
    this.cache.delete(evaluator.name);
  }

  get(name: string): IFlagEvaluator | undefined {
    if (!this.cache.has(name)) {
      this.cache.set(name, this.evaluators.get(name));
    }
    return this.cache.get(name);
  }

  evaluate(config: FlagConfig, ctx: FlagEvaluationContext): boolean {
    const evaluator = this.get(config.strategy);
    if (!evaluator) throw new Error(`Unknown strategy: ${config.strategy}`);
    return evaluator.evaluate(config, ctx);
  }
}

// Built-in evaluators
const BooleanEvaluator: IFlagEvaluator = {
  name: 'Boolean',
  evaluate: (config) => config.parameters?.value === true,
};

const PercentageEvaluator: IFlagEvaluator = {
  name: 'Percentage',
  evaluate: (config, ctx) => {
    const percentage = config.parameters?.percentage ?? 0;
    const hash = this.hash(ctx.userId ?? Math.random().toString());
    return (hash % 100) < percentage;
  },
  hash: (str: string) => str.split('').reduce((a, b) => ((a << 5) - a) + b.charCodeAt(0), 0),
};

// Usage
const registry = new FlagRegistry();
registry.register(BooleanEvaluator);
registry.register(PercentageEvaluator);

const result = registry.evaluate(
  { key: 'new-feature', strategy: 'Percentage', parameters: { percentage: 25 } },
  { userId: 'user-123' }
);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a robust, runtime-extensible scheduling system that supports multiple job types and dynamic plug-ins without touching core executor.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for scheduling policies
- Registry/Plugin architecture for runtime extension
- Concurrency, thread-safety, and asynchronous dispatch
- Type-safe factories and minimal interfaces in TypeScript

## Code Example
```typescript
type JobType = 'cpu'|'io'|'delay'
interface Job {
  id: string
  type: JobType
  priority?: number
  payload?: any
  run: () => Promise<void>
}
interface Scheduler {
  schedule(job: Job): Promise<void>
}
type Factory = () => Scheduler
class SchedulerRegistry {
  private static factories = new Map<string, Factory>()
  static register(name: string, factory: Factory){ this.factories.set(name, factory) }
  static create(name: string): Scheduler {
    const f = this.factories.get(name)
    if (!f) throw new Error(`Unknown scheduler: ${name}`)
    return f()
  }
}
class PriorityScheduler implements Scheduler {
  private queue: Job[] = []
  async schedule(job: Job): Promise<void> {
    this.queue.push(job)
    this.queue.sort((a,b)=> (b.priority ?? 0) - (a.priority ?? 0))
    // pretend execution
    const head = this.queue.shift()
    if (head) await head.run()
  }
}
// Wiring
SchedulerRegistry.register('priority', ()=> new PriorityScheduler())
```

